---
import PathwayLandingLayout from '../../layouts/PathwayLandingLayout.astro';
import { getPathwayHero } from '@/lib/pathwayHeroes';
import { SITE_CONFIG } from '@/lib/seo/config';

const title = 'EB-5 for Indian International Students | StudentEB5';
const metaDescription =
  'EB-5 for Indian international students explains how F-1 students can evaluate a self-sponsored green card path, including parental funding, timing, and how it compares to the H-1B route.';
const canonicalUrl = `${SITE_CONFIG.url}/pathways/eb5-for-indian-students`;
const h1 = 'EB-5 for Indian International Students';
const subhead =
  'A self-sponsored path to a U.S. green card for Indian F-1 students (independent of the H-1B lottery and employer sponsorship).';

const relatedResearch = [
  {
    title: 'H-1B vs EB-5 Lifetime Cost: Indian and Chinese Students',
    url: '/research/comparisons/h1b-vs-eb5-lifetime-cost-indian-chinese-students',
    description: 'Lifetime earnings and visa-path tradeoffs for students deciding between H-1B and EB-5.',
  },
  {
    title: 'EB-5 for Indian Students and Chinese Students on F-1 and STEM OPT | Analysis of the Cap-Gap Strategy',
    url: '/research/f1-students/eb5-indian-chinese-students-f1-stem-opt',
    description: 'How STEM OPT timing intersects with EB-5 planning for Indian and Chinese students.',
  },
];
const hero = getPathwayHero('eb5-for-indian-students');

---

<PathwayLandingLayout
  title={title}
  metaDescription={metaDescription}
  canonicalUrl={canonicalUrl}
  h1={h1}
  subhead={subhead}
  heroImage={hero.image}
  heroImageWebp={hero.webp}
  heroImageAlt="EB-5 pathway for Indian students"
  relatedResearch={relatedResearch}
  secondaryCtaText="Talk to Someone Who Understands the F-1 to EB-5 Path"
  breadcrumbTitle="EB-5 for Indian Students"
  keywords={[
    'EB-5 for Indian international students',
    'Indian F-1 EB-5',
    'EB-5 parental funding India',
    'F-1 to EB-5 India',
  ]}
>
  <h2>The two paths after graduation</h2>
  <div class="not-prose grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-6 mb-6">
    <div class="rounded-lg border border-border bg-card p-6">
      <h3 class="font-serif text-xl font-semibold text-foreground mb-3">The Traditional Path</h3>
      <p class="text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed m-0" style="line-height: 1.75;">
        F-1 → Graduation → OPT / STEM OPT → H-1B lottery → Employment-based green card queue (historically long waits for
        many Indian-born applicants in EB-2/EB-3)
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="rounded-lg border border-border bg-card p-6">
      <h3 class="font-serif text-xl font-semibold text-foreground mb-3">The EB-5 Path</h3>
      <p class="text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed m-0" style="line-height: 1.75;">
        F-1 → EB-5 investment and I-526E filing → Concurrent filing for work authorization and travel document →
        Potential self-sponsored green card, independent of employer or lottery outcome
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div
    class="not-prose rounded-lg border-2 border-foreground/20 bg-secondary/50 p-5 mb-8"
    role="note"
  >
    <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed m-0" style="line-height: 1.7;">
      EB-5 does not automatically or immediately convert F-1 status into a green card. Filing an EB-5 petition is a
      separate immigration process with its own eligibility, documentation, and timeline requirements. This comparison
      is educational. Talk with a qualified immigration attorney about your specific situation.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>Why Indian F-1 students look at EB-5</h2>
  <p>
    Indian students on F-1 status face two separate sources of uncertainty after graduation: the H-1B lottery, which is
    not guaranteed, and employment-based green card categories, where India-chargeable EB-2 and EB-3 cases have
    historically faced significant backlogs. Exact wait times change and should be checked against the current
    <a
      href="https://travel.state.gov/content/travel/en/legal/visa-law0/visa-bulletin.html"
      target="_blank"
      rel="noopener noreferrer">Visa Bulletin</a
    >, not assumed from older figures. EB-5 is structured differently. It does not depend on lottery selection or on an
    employer's willingness to sponsor a petition. For students whose families have the financial capacity to consider
    it, some begin evaluating EB-5 while still in school, which allows more time to document source of funds and select
    a project before OPT or STEM OPT authorization ends. For the broader student pathway overview, see
    <a href="/pathways/f1-to-eb5-self-sponsored-green-card">F-1 to EB-5 self-sponsored green card</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>How parental funding usually works</h2>
  <p>
    Many Indian F-1 students are not the source of investment capital themselves. A parent or family member often
    provides the funds, typically as a documented gift. USCIS requires a clear, lawful paper trail for any funds used
    in an EB-5 investment, regardless of who originally earned them. Families remitting capital from India also need to
    plan around RBI and Liberalised Remittance Scheme (LRS) compliance with a qualified adviser. See our full guide on
    <a href="/pathways/eb5-for-indian-families"
      >how Indian families can fund an EB-5 investment</a
    >.
  </p>

  <h2>Timing considerations</h2>
  <p>
    EB-5 program rules and filing windows change. Students evaluating this path should confirm current deadlines,
    investment thresholds, and visa category rules before making a decision. See our
    <a href="/tools/grandfathering-countdown">Grandfathering Deadline tool</a> and
    <a href="/research/complete-2027-eb5-guide">current EB-5 guide</a> for the latest information. Country-specific STEM
    OPT timing risks are covered in
    <a href="/research/f1-students/eb5-indian-chinese-students-f1-stem-opt">EB-5 for Indian and Chinese students on F-1 and STEM OPT</a>.
  </p>
</PathwayLandingLayout>
